.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NFANON 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nfanon
.Nd anonymize the IP addresses 
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm 
.Fl r Ar path
.Op Fl w Ar nffile
.Fl K Ar key | Fl k Ar file
.Op Fl C Ar config
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl N Ar file
.Op Fl T
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl t Ar num
.Op Fl v Ar level
.Op Fl W
.Op Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
anonymizes all IP addresses ( src, dst, next hop, router IP, NAT, tunnel endpoints
etc. ) in the netflow records using the CryptoPAn (Cryptography-based Prefix-preserving
Anonymization) module. Raw packet data, such as sampled frames and payload, is cleared. The key -K is used to initialize the Rijndael cipher. The key is either 
a 32 character string, or a 64 hex digit string starting with 0x. See 
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crypto-PAn for more information on CryptoPAn.
.Pp
The source specified by argument
.Fl r Ar path
may point to a single nfdump file or to a directory containing many nfdump files.
All files in a directory are processed recursively.
.Pp
If the output argument
.Fl w Ar nffile
is given, all anonymized records are written into that single file, even if the 
source is a directory. If no argument
.Fl w
is specified, 
.Nm
overwrites the original source file with the anonymized flow records.
If the source is a directory, each flow file is anonymized respectively.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r Ar path
Path to read flow files to anonymize. Path may point to a single file or a directory
containing many flow files.
.It Fl w Ar nffile
File name to write anonymized flow records to. If this argument is missing, the source
file name is taken, which means the original file is overwritten.
.Nm
writes into a new temporary file
.Ar nffile Ns -XXXXXX
in the same directory, with a unique random suffix, and renames it to
.Ar nffile
when complete. Without
.Fl w
the temporary file is named after the input file. An existing file is
replaced, never written into, so
.Ar nffile
may name the input file, or a hard or symbolic link to it.
.It Fl K Ar key
.Ar key
is either a 32 character string, or a 64 char hex string starting with 0x. This key
is used to initialize the anonymizer.
.It Fl k Ar file
Read the key from
.Ar file
instead of the command line, where every local user can see it in the process
list. The file contains the key in the same format as for
.Fl K ,
trailing white space and newlines are ignored.
.Fl K
and
.Fl k
are mutually exclusive.
.It Fl v Ar level
Set verbose level. A verbose level of 0 disables the activity spinner.
.It Fl W Ar num
Set the number of worker threads. The default depends on the number of cores online.
The value must not exceed the number of online CPU cores.
.Pp
Worker threads are used to anonymize flow records in parallel.
.It Fl C Ar config
Read configuration from config; use
.Sy none
to suppress configuration-file loading.
.It Fl s
Preserve source addresses rather than anonymising them.
.It Fl d
Preserve destination addresses rather than anonymising them.
.It Fl N Ar file
Anonymize only addresses in the networks listed in
.Ar file .
The file contains one IPv4 or IPv6 network in CIDR notation per line. A bare
address is a single host. A line starting with
.Sy !
lists a network to preserve. Blank lines and text after
.Sy #
are ignored. Each address is checked against the most specific matching
network: it is anonymized only if that network is not a preserve network.
Addresses that match no network are left unchanged.
This applies to all addresses, including next hop, BGP next hop, router IP,
NAT, tunnel and exporter addresses.
.Fl s
and
.Fl d
still apply to the source and destination side. The source AS and previous
adjacent AS are set to 0 only if the source address was anonymized, the
destination AS and next adjacent AS only if the destination address was.
.Pp
IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses (::ffff:0:0/96) are checked against the IPv4
networks. A mapped network ::ffff:a.b.c.d/n with n >= 96 in
.Ar file
is the IPv4 network a.b.c.d/n-96. An IPv6 network, which covers the mapped
range without being part of it, such as ::/0, applies to the other IPv6
addresses only.
.It Fl T
Mark the anonymized sides of each flow in the two low bits of the source ToS
byte. The bits are cleared, then bit 1 (value 2) is set if the source address
was anonymized, bit 0 (value 1) if the destination address was. Without
.Fl N
every side not preserved by
.Fl s
or
.Fl d
counts as anonymized. Only the source and destination flow addresses set the
bits. Records without a ToS field are not marked.
.It Fl q
Deprecated compatibility option; it is rejected. Use
.Fl v 0
for quiet logging.
.It Fl t Ar num
Legacy alias for
.Fl W Ar num .
.It Fl h
Print help text to stdout and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
returns 0 on success, and 1 or 255 otherwise. Any error is fatal: a record or block
that can not be anonymized, a corrupt, truncated or unreadable input file, data
blocks the file header does not count, bytes after the last block, or an
output file that can not be written or closed completely, for example on a full disk.
.Pp
On a fatal error, and when terminated by SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP or SIGABRT,
.Nm
removes the incomplete output file and never replaces an input file in place.
Other signals, such as SIGKILL, may leave a temporary file
.Ar nffile Ns -XXXXXX
behind, but never replace an input file or the file named by
.Fl w .
Use the
output file only if
.Nm
returned 0.
.Sh EXAMPLES
To create a random 64 character hex string you may use the following command:
.Dl % xxd -u -l 32 -p -c 64 /dev/urandom
.Pp
Use the resulting output as key, prepended with 0x as -K argument.
.Pp
Or
.Dl % openssl rand -hex 16
.Pp
and use this output as argument for -K
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nfdump 1
